{
  "summary": "Wraps `fclose()` calls in `src/logging.cpp` with a helper that checks return values and reports errors directly to `stderr`. The helper includes the file path and `SysErrorString(errno)` to avoid re-entering the logger or acquiring locks on failure. It replaces raw `fclose()` invocations in test logger disconnection, debug log reopening, and log file shrinking.",
  "problem": "Errors encountered when closing log files—such as write failures or disk-full conditions when flushing buffered output during `debug.log` shrinking—are silently ignored. Operators receive no warning when closing fails and log output is dropped.",
  "evidence": [
    "Author description: \"`fclose()` can report write errors (for example if the disk is full or the filesystem has a problem). Right now, some `fclose()` calls in `src/logging.cpp` ignore the return value. This means errors might go unnoticed and log lines could be lost without warning.\"",
    "Commit 88d5c0b467: \"Log-file close failures currently go unreported, including failures to flush buffered output when shrinking debug.log.\""
  ],
